        "original_id": "3984a205-0ade-5bd8-bc88-445cb7f2adb2",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: John Wesley, Charles Wesley, George Whitefield, Edward Perronet\n- Organizations: Church of England\n- Places: England, Melbourne, Victoria, Australia\n- Roles: Anglican cleric, theologian, Anglican priest\n- Concepts: Methodism, eighteenth-century revival\n- Buildings: St Peter's, Eastern Hill\n\nFacts:\n- Methodism originated as a revival within the 18th-century Church of England.\n- John Wesley was born on 28 June 1703 old style 17 June 1703 and died on 2 March 1791.\n- John Wesley was an English Anglican cleric and theologian who, with his brother Charles Wesley and fellow cleric George Whitefield, founded Methodism.\n- Methodism became a separate denomination after John Wesley's death.\n- Edward Perronet was born in 1726 and died on 2 January 1792.\n- Edward Perronet was the son of an Anglican priest and worked closely with Anglican priest John Wesley and Charles Wesley for many years in England's eighteenth-century revival.\n- St Peter's, Eastern Hill is the Anglican parish church of the City of Melbourne in Victoria, Australia.",
